{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>I love my master, my wife, and my children; I shall not go out free, etc.</b> The reason for piercing the ear was stated by Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai: the ear that heard at Mount Sinai ״לֹא תִגְנֹב״ and went and stole — let it be pierced, etc. (Mekhilta ad loc.). And the question is raised upon this: why should he not be pierced immediately, when the court sells him or when he sells himself? And in my opinion it is far more fitting to understand that he is struck in his ear for the reason derived in Bava Kamma, chapter Merubah (74b–75a): Why did the Torah deal more strictly with the thief than with the robber? Because the thief did not give equal weight to the dignity of a servant and the dignity of his master — as it were, he treated the Eye above as though it does not see and the Ear above as though it does not hear, etc. Therefore it is fitting that he be struck in his ear, because he sinned against the Ear above. And with this the question is resolved — why this one is pierced more than any other wrongdoer: for according to the words of Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai it is difficult, for on every transgression in the Torah he should be pierced. And one should not ask, if so he should be struck also in his eye — for if so, he would blind him and he would not be fit to work. But one who sells himself because of his poverty has another reason in the matter: for all the while that he is a servant of servants, he must incline his ear to listen to the voice of his master, and he removes his ear from listening to the voice of HaShem — for another's authority is upon him — which is not so with one who is sold on account of his theft, for he is coerced in the matter, since the court sells him." "Nevertheless it is difficult: why should the thief be pierced immediately, even if he has means to pay? And likewise the one who sells himself — should he be pierced immediately at the time of sale? The answer to this: we do not judge a man with two judgments, for any thief, if he has the means, pays double, and then he has received his sentence and is discharged. And if the court sold him for his theft, his punishment is servitude. And likewise one who sells himself because of his poverty — he too is coerced, driven by the great distress that brought him to such a measure. But against this too, the Torah gave him an opening to go out to freedom after six years, and that is by his own free will without any coercion whatsoever. And if ״אָמֹר יֹאמַר הָעֶבֶד אָהַבְתִּי אֶת אֲדֹנִי אֶת אִשְׁתִּי וְאֶת בָּנָי לֹא אֵצֵא חָפְשִׁי״ (Exodus 21:5) — and behold, he chooses to be a servant of servants and removes his ear from listening to the voice of HaShem — then it is fitting that he be struck in his ear. And the one sold on account of his theft also requires a punishment other than servitude, for it is evident in retrospect that servitude is no punishment at all for him, since he still chooses servitude, as it says ״טוֹב לוֹ עִמָּךְ״ (Deuteronomy 15:16). And where is his punishment for ״לֹא תִּגְנֹב״ (Exodus 20:13), and for ״כִּי לִי בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל עֲבָדִים״ — and not servants of servants (Bava Kamma 116b)? Therefore he is pierced in the ear, for by this he is freed from these two transgressions, for one punishment applies to both. And this reason suffices according to the words of Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai, and it also resolves why he should not be pierced immediately, even according to his words." "And the reason for the piercing specifically at the door and at the doorpost: because after six years the Torah opened for him an opening to go out from servitude to freedom, and the door turns on its hinge and the servant does not wish to go out — like one who sits in a prison and they open a door for him, saying to him: go, flee for your life! — yet so does he choose the imagined goods that he possesses, for it is good for him there in food and drink. Therefore he leaves the door open and does not wish to go out; it is fitting that he be pierced at the door or upon the doorpost, upon which is inscribed the passage of ״וְאָהַבְתָּ אֵת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ (Deuteronomy 6:5), while he says ״אָהַבְתִּי אֶת אֲדֹנִי אֶת אִשְׁתִּי וְאֶת בָּנָי״ (Exodus 21:5) — and behold, he exchanges the love of HaShem, blessed be He, for the love of his maidservant wife; therefore he is pierced at the doorpost. And further: every free man has it in his power to fulfill what is said (Proverbs 8:34) ״לִשְׁקֹד עַל דַּלְתֹתַי יוֹם יוֹם לִשְׁמֹר מְזוּזוֹת פְּתָחָי״ — yet this one waits upon the doors of his master and removes his ear from listening to the voice of HaShem, to wait upon His doors; therefore he is pierced there, to say to him: behold, by your own evil choice you are fixed here and shall never again move from this place." "And there is yet another reason for the piercing of the ear, along the lines of what our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Kiddushin 70a): whoever marries a woman who is unfit for him — Elijah binds him and the Holy One, blessed be He, pierces him. Although Rashi explained ״רוֹצְעוֹ״ as 'strikes him with a strap,' nevertheless it seems to me more correct to say ״רוֹצְעוֹ״ — with an awl. And this matter we learn from the law of this servant who says ״אָהַבְתִּי אֶת אִשְׁתִּי״ — the maidservant who is unfit: for even though the Torah gave his master permission to give him a Canaanite maidservant, nevertheless against this too the Torah gave him an opening to remove from himself this shame and reproach and to go out from her to freedom. But when he says ״אָהַבְתִּי אֶת אִשְׁתִּי״ the maidservant and does not wish to go out from her, then it is as though at that moment, of his own free will, he marries this unfit woman; therefore he is struck in his ear, for no limb feels this disgrace like the ear when it hears the gossip of all those around saying, 'This is your unfit wife and these are your blemished children.' And from here this law goes forth for all who marry an unfit woman." "And there is yet another reason for the piercing of the ear: for truly he should be struck over his entire body, but the Torah had pity on him, that he be struck in one limb equivalent to his entire body. For one who cut off his fellow's hand pays him the value of his hand; one who blinded his eye pays the value of his eye; one who deafened him pays the value of his entire body (Bava Kamma 85a) — and so it is concluded in Sha'arei Teshuvah of Rabbenu Yonah. Beyond all these reasons, there is in this matter a fitting allusion for all the blind ones in the camp of the Hebrews, who bear all their days upon their shoulders every kind of servitude and affliction and pain in order to acquire imagined possessions — and to those possessions he is sold as an eternal servant. And he claims that he is compelled to labor in silver and gold for the sake of his wife and children, as if he toils for their sake and does not seek to go out to freedom all his days. And he willingly accepts upon himself all the afflictions, saying ״אָהַבְתִּי אֶת אִשְׁתִּי וְאֶת בָּנָי לֹא אֵצֵא חָפְשִׁי״ (Exodus 21:5) — ״חָרוּת חֵרוּת עַל הַלּוּחוֹת״ (Avot 6:2) — for here I am bound with human ropes and with the bonds of love for wife and children. And therefore he is called an enslaved servant (עֶבֶד נִרְצָע), for all the days of his life the man shall not rest until he has consumed his vain days and years in panic and diversions, one after another; and when he dies, his wife and children will not escort him except to the door of his grave, and from there they part — as is found in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer in the parable of the three friends, etc. (chapter 34). Therefore it is said of this servant without qualification: ״וְהִגִּישׁוֹ אֲדֹנָיו אֶל הָאֱלֹהִים״ (Exodus 21:6) — to the place of judgment. For herein is an allusion to every man: that in the future, on the Day of Judgment, his wife and children — concerning whom he said ״אָהַבְתִּי אֶת אִשְׁתִּי וְאֶת בָּנָי״ — will not come to his aid, for they do not love him so very much; and on the contrary, they will abandon him from the door onward, and there crouches sin at the door, and from the door onward they shall not enter with him to plead on his behalf. And this is a fitting allusion and a secret from the hidden mysteries of the Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:39.816792621Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}